WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:07.200
And now the use case is about maintaining an audit logs.

00:07.400 --> 00:08.640
So what do we mean with that?

00:08.680 --> 00:10.840
Let's have for example the table employees.

00:11.080 --> 00:16.880
The employee data are usually very sensitive informations because there we can see which employees are

00:16.920 --> 00:17.480
added.

00:17.480 --> 00:20.440
The salary updates the employee terminations.

00:20.560 --> 00:26.280
And this makes the table very important because we would like to track all those changes that is happening

00:26.320 --> 00:27.160
to this table.

00:27.160 --> 00:32.840
So each time we are inserting, updating deleting we would like to maintain a log about all those changes.

00:32.960 --> 00:34.760
In order to analyze it later.

00:34.760 --> 00:39.400
It is of course very important, such as logs for the compliance and the auditors.

00:39.520 --> 00:45.480
And in case there is like a problem, we can go to the logs to understand when this happened, who made

00:45.480 --> 00:47.640
the changes and what exactly changed.

00:47.680 --> 00:51.800
And now in order to maintain logs, we can use the power of triggers.

00:51.960 --> 00:57.200
So what we're going to do, we're going to go and attach like a trigger on the table employees.

00:57.240 --> 01:02.880
And each time we insert a new data to the employees we are triggering another events.

01:03.000 --> 01:09.380
So what's going to happen This new employee can be inserted in the audit logs in order to have a record

01:09.380 --> 01:11.140
about this activity in the logs.

01:11.180 --> 01:17.500
So that means each time you are inserting data to the table employees, you are automatically inserting

01:17.500 --> 01:18.980
data inside the logs.

01:19.020 --> 01:21.180
And this is really amazing use case for the triggers.

01:21.180 --> 01:22.780
So let's go and implement it.

01:22.820 --> 01:23.020
Okay.

01:23.060 --> 01:25.460
So now let's check quickly the syntax of that triggers.

01:25.460 --> 01:28.860
So we start with the usual create trigger then the trigger name.

01:28.860 --> 01:33.140
And then we have to specify on which table this trigger can be built in.

01:33.140 --> 01:36.780
So now we are attaching like a trigger on top of one table.

01:36.780 --> 01:41.420
And after that we have to define for the SQL when this trigger is going to happen.

01:41.420 --> 01:43.860
So what is actually triggering the trigger.

01:43.900 --> 01:49.180
And here you can define after or instead then you have to define the operator.

01:49.180 --> 01:52.140
So first you have to define like after or instead of.

01:52.180 --> 01:54.540
And then we have to define the operation.

01:54.540 --> 01:56.980
So insert update delete or one of them.

01:56.980 --> 02:00.180
And with that you are telling SQL when exactly this should happen.

02:00.180 --> 02:05.380
And now after that we have to tell SQL what can happen if the trigger is triggered.

02:05.380 --> 02:10.930
So here we have like begin and end and then we have like several SQL statements that's going to describe

02:10.930 --> 02:12.970
what can happen once we have that trigger.

02:13.010 --> 02:13.970
So that's it.

02:13.970 --> 02:16.210
As you can see the syntax is very simple.

02:16.250 --> 02:16.450
Okay.

02:16.490 --> 02:18.050
So now let's do it step by step.

02:18.090 --> 02:22.330
First I would like to create a table where we can store the logs information.

02:22.330 --> 02:23.650
So it's going to be very simple table.

02:23.650 --> 02:25.290
We're going to say create table.

02:25.290 --> 02:29.370
Then we're going to call it sales employee logs.

02:29.570 --> 02:31.730
And we're going to have the following columns inside it.

02:32.250 --> 02:33.570
So let's start with the primary key.

02:33.570 --> 02:36.330
It's going to be the log ID and the data type int.

02:36.330 --> 02:37.890
And then we're going to have like a sequence.

02:37.890 --> 02:39.370
So we're going to have identity.

02:39.410 --> 02:41.650
And this is the primary key.

02:42.170 --> 02:43.090
Let's go to the next one.

02:43.090 --> 02:46.650
It's going to be the employee ID and the data type going to be int.

02:46.690 --> 02:49.090
The next one is going to be the log message.

02:49.330 --> 02:50.850
So let's have it as a varchar.

02:51.290 --> 02:54.130
And I'm going to have it like 255.

02:54.170 --> 02:56.930
And then to the next one we're going to have the log dates.

02:56.970 --> 02:59.850
And then we're going to have like let's say a date or a date time.

02:59.850 --> 03:00.770
So that's it.

03:00.770 --> 03:02.370
Let's go and execute it.

03:02.370 --> 03:05.010
And with that we have a new table inside our database.

03:05.010 --> 03:08.330
Now the next step is that we're going to go and create our trigger.

03:08.330 --> 03:10.610
So we're going to say create trigger.

03:10.910 --> 03:12.950
and I'm going to call it like this DRG.

03:13.110 --> 03:19.710
It is just a prefix to indicate this is a trigger, and I'm just going to kill it after insert employee.

03:20.590 --> 03:25.070
And now we have to define the table so it can be on sales employee.

03:25.950 --> 03:29.790
So now with that we are saying we have now a trigger on the table employees.

03:29.830 --> 03:31.310
And now we have to define the logic.

03:31.310 --> 03:33.670
So we're going to use after insert.

03:34.030 --> 03:39.790
So that means after we insert any record to the table employees the following things should happen.

03:39.790 --> 03:43.350
So we're going to say as and then begin and end.

03:43.710 --> 03:45.790
And in between we can have our logic.

03:45.790 --> 03:49.550
So what can happen after a new record is inserted to the employees.

03:49.550 --> 03:53.150
We're going to go and insert a new record to the employee logs.

03:53.150 --> 03:57.430
So we're going to have insert into sales employee logs.

03:58.670 --> 04:07.150
And we're going to have here the three columns employee ID, the log message and the log dates.

04:07.150 --> 04:08.790
So now which value is going to be inserted.

04:08.790 --> 04:10.550
It's going to be like from a query.

04:10.550 --> 04:11.870
So we're going to say select.

04:11.870 --> 04:18.370
And we're going to say as well employee ID and for the log message we can have customized one like let's

04:18.370 --> 04:25.010
say a new employee added and it's going to be equal to the employee ID.

04:25.010 --> 04:28.210
So in order to have the employee ID it's going to be like this.

04:29.450 --> 04:30.490
So that's it.

04:30.810 --> 04:31.690
Now to the next one.

04:31.690 --> 04:33.330
We need the log date.

04:33.370 --> 04:34.850
It's going to be get date.

04:35.730 --> 04:39.450
And now you might say okay but where this employee ID is coming from.

04:39.690 --> 04:43.210
Well it's going to come from the table from inserted.

04:43.530 --> 04:45.130
So what is actually inserted.

04:45.130 --> 04:51.530
It is like special virtual table that holds all the new inserted data to our table employees.

04:51.530 --> 04:56.610
So anything we are inserting inside the employees will be available inside this table.

04:56.650 --> 05:01.370
And of course this is only available during the execution of this trigger.

05:01.370 --> 05:06.490
So you cannot go now outside of this query and start querying the table inserted because you will not

05:06.490 --> 05:07.330
find anything.

05:07.330 --> 05:13.250
This is only like a virtual table that contains anything that you are doing to the table employees.

05:13.250 --> 05:17.250
And you find a lot of informations like the salary, the age and so on.

05:17.290 --> 05:19.270
So that's it for the inserted.

05:19.310 --> 05:24.830
Now we have to make sure that in our message we have everything as a string because the employee ID

05:24.830 --> 05:25.710
is an integer.

05:25.710 --> 05:26.950
So we have to cast it.

05:26.950 --> 05:27.750
So cast.

05:27.790 --> 05:32.870
And then we're going to say as var char like this.

05:32.910 --> 05:34.270
Otherwise we'll get an error.

05:34.310 --> 05:36.270
So I think we have our trigger ready.

05:36.310 --> 05:39.110
We have a new trigger on the table employees.

05:39.150 --> 05:42.430
And now the first question is when this trigger is going to happen.

05:42.550 --> 05:46.670
Well it can happen after inserting data to the employees.

05:46.710 --> 05:49.230
And then the second question what's going to happen?

05:49.310 --> 05:55.030
Well, once we have this event, the whole thing here is going to be executed where we are saying insert

05:55.030 --> 06:00.550
to the logs, the employee ID, the message, and as well the date when this happens.

06:00.550 --> 06:04.950
And we can get all those informations from the table, the virtual table inserted.

06:04.990 --> 06:06.110
So I think we are ready.

06:06.110 --> 06:07.750
Let's go and execute it.

06:07.790 --> 06:13.750
And now if you go to the Object explorer to our database let's go to our table employees and then to

06:13.790 --> 06:14.510
the triggers.

06:14.510 --> 06:19.430
So if you refresh over here you can see our new trigger that we just created.

06:19.510 --> 06:22.340
So with that we have defined our trigger and we are ready.

06:22.380 --> 06:26.020
Now the next step is that we're going to go and trigger our trigger.

06:26.020 --> 06:27.140
So let's go and do that.

06:27.140 --> 06:28.340
Let's have a new query.

06:28.540 --> 06:31.140
But first I'm going to have a look to our logs.

06:32.420 --> 06:35.860
So sales employee logs.

06:36.060 --> 06:37.300
So let's query this one.

06:37.300 --> 06:42.380
And as you can see our logs is empty because we didn't insert anything to the table employees.

06:42.420 --> 06:43.460
Let's go and do that.

06:43.500 --> 06:44.740
Let's trigger our trigger.

06:44.740 --> 06:49.460
So what we can do we're going to say insert into sales employees.

06:49.460 --> 06:51.420
And we're going to have the following values.

06:51.420 --> 06:53.940
So we are at the counter I think six.

06:54.020 --> 06:58.340
Let's have the first name Maria the last name.

06:58.620 --> 07:00.780
And then we're going to have the position.

07:00.780 --> 07:04.340
It's going to be the HR for example the birth dates.

07:04.580 --> 07:07.020
Let's pick something I don't know.

07:07.060 --> 07:11.100
We have a female here and the salary.

07:11.100 --> 07:13.460
Let's go and get this salary.

07:13.460 --> 07:15.780
And the hierarchy is going to be for example three.

07:15.780 --> 07:17.820
So let's go and execute it.

07:17.820 --> 07:21.180
And with that as you can see we have inserted a new data to the employees.

07:21.380 --> 07:22.780
Let's check now the logs.

07:22.980 --> 07:24.920
So All, let's query it.

07:24.960 --> 07:27.760
So we have here a nice log about the employee number six.

07:28.080 --> 07:29.680
And we have a nice message.

07:29.680 --> 07:36.400
And when this did happen of course you can go and insert another employee let's say seven for the same

07:36.400 --> 07:36.960
data.

07:36.960 --> 07:39.040
So let's do that and check the logs.

07:39.360 --> 07:42.640
And with that we have another log for the new employee.

07:42.640 --> 07:44.160
So this is really amazing use case.

07:44.160 --> 07:46.560
In order to maintain a log to your data.

07:46.560 --> 07:51.160
And you can go and make like some analysis on how many inserts did happens.

07:51.320 --> 07:52.920
And of course not only on the inserts.

07:52.920 --> 07:55.040
You can have it on the update delete.

07:55.240 --> 07:56.760
So as you can see it is very simple.

07:56.760 --> 07:58.800
This is how you create a triggers in SQL.

07:58.840 --> 07:59.600
All right my friends.

07:59.600 --> 08:03.360
So that's all about the triggers with that with with that we have covered.

08:03.360 --> 08:08.760
Now with that we have covered now all the concepts and topics that you have to learn about SQL.

08:08.840 --> 08:11.560
Now in the next chapter it's going to be about the performance.

08:11.560 --> 08:17.120
So as you start writing queries and so on you will start noticing some queries are really slow.

08:17.160 --> 08:21.240
Now what we're going to do in this chapter, we're going to learn different techniques on how to optimize

08:21.240 --> 08:22.120
the performance.

08:22.120 --> 08:26.880
And the first and the very famous one is to go and build indexes in databases.

08:26.880 --> 08:28.280
So let's understand what this means.
